OAI_PAPER({"slug": "A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026", "title": "A continuum temperature singularity for a radial pair potential", "pdf": "https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/A-continuum-temperature-singularity-for-a-radial-pair-potential-September-24-2026/paper.pdf", "family": "228", "family_title": "Continuum phase transitions for radial pair potentials", "abstract_html": "We construct a stable radial pair potential in three dimensions whose canonical free energy has a strict downward derivative jump at one common finite positive inverse temperature throughout an open interval of positive densities. The potential has a divergent repulsive core, a nontrivial attractive interval, and an integrable tail satisfying \\(\\phi(r)=o(r^{-3})\\). Its free-cube canonical free energy is finite at every positive inverse temperature and density.", "blocks": [{"label": "Theorem 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Common canonical temperature singularity (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Common canonical temperature singularity (paper: Theorem 1.1).**</p><p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">A radial difference-only pair potential on \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) is written\n  \\[\n  \\Phi(x)=\\varphi(|x|).\n  \\]\n  It is called admissible if it is Borel measurable, \\(\\Phi(0)=+\\infty\\), \\(\\varphi\\) is finite and locally bounded on \\((0,\\infty)\\), and:\n  \\[\n  \\varphi(r)\\longrightarrow+\\infty\\qquad(r\\downarrow0);\n  \\]\n  there are \\(0&lt;r_1&lt;r_2\\) and \\(a&gt;0\\) such that\n  \\[\n  \\varphi(r)\\le-a\\qquad(r\\in[r_1,r_2]);\n  \\]\n  for some finite \\(R\\),\n  \\[\n  \\int_R^\\infty r^2|\\varphi(r)|\\,dr&lt;\\infty;\n  \\]\n  and there is \\(B&lt;\\infty\\) such that every finite configuration satisfies\n  \\[\n  U_N^\\Phi(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)\n  :=\\sum_{1\\le i&lt;j\\le N}\\Phi(x_i-x_j)\\ge-BN.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Lambda_L=[-L/2,L/2]^3\\). With free boundary conditions, define\n  \\[\n  Z_{\\Lambda,N}^\\Phi(\\beta)\n  =\\frac1{N!}\\int_{\\Lambda^N}\n  e^{-\\beta U_N^\\Phi(x_1,\\ldots,x_N)}\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_N,\n  \\qquad \\beta&gt;0,\n  \\]\n  with \\(Z_{\\Lambda,0}^\\Phi=1\\) and \\(e^{-\\beta\\infty}=0\\). The position measure is Lebesgue measure, independent of \\(\\beta\\).</p><p class=\"li\">When the limit exists, define\n  \\[\n  f_\\Phi(\\beta,\\rho)\n  =-\\frac1\\beta\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\n  L^{-3}\\log Z_{\\Lambda_L,\\lfloor\\rho L^3\\rfloor}^\\Phi(\\beta).\n  \\]\n  Write \\(D_\\beta^-\\) and \\(D_\\beta^+\\) for left and right derivatives in inverse temperature.</p><p>Statement. There exist an admissible radial difference-only pair potential \\(\\Phi\\), a nonempty open interval \\(I\\subset(0,\\infty)\\), and one\n\\[\n\\beta_c\\in(1/2,3/2)\n\\]\nsuch that:</p><p class=\"li num\">1. \\(\\varphi(r)=o(r^{-3})\\) as \\(r\\to\\infty\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">2. the free-cube canonical limit exists and is finite for every \\(\\beta&gt;0\\) and every \\(\\rho&gt;0\\);</p><p class=\"li num\">3. for every \\(\\rho\\in I\\), the finite one-sided derivatives satisfy\n   \\[\n   D_\\beta^-f_\\Phi(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n   &gt;\n   D_\\beta^+f_\\Phi(\\beta_c,\\rho).\n   \\]</p><p>Thus one potential and one critical inverse temperature give a strict canonical temperature singularity throughout \\(I\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Take the potential \\(\\Phi\\), pressure \\(Q_\\infty\\), canonical function \\(H_\\infty\\), and point \\(\\lambda_c=(\\beta_c,h_c)\\) from Proposition \\(13\\). Admissibility and \\(\\varphi(r)=o(r^{-3})\\) are already established. Proposition \\(3\\) gives the finite canonical limit for every positive inverse temperature and density.</p><p>The center density of the preserved slope ball is\n\\[\n\\rho_* = g_2=\\frac{0+p+4p}{3}=\\frac{5p}{3}&gt;0.\n\\]\nSet\n\\[\n\\delta_\\rho=\\min\\{\\gamma/2,\\rho_*/2\\},\n\\qquad\nI=(\\rho_*-\\delta_\\rho,\\rho_*+\\delta_\\rho).\n\\]\nThis is a nonempty open interval in \\((0,\\infty)\\).</p><p>Fix \\(\\rho\\in I\\). The vectors\n\\[\nw_-=(g_1-\\gamma/2,\\rho),\n\\qquad\nw_+=(g_1+\\gamma/2,\\rho)\n\\]\nhave distance less than \\(\\gamma\\) from \\(g\\), so both belong to\n\\[\n\\partial Q_\\infty(\\beta_c,h_c).\n\\]</p><p>For any \\((s,\\rho)\\) in this subdifferential, restricting the supporting inequality to \\(\\beta=\\beta_c\\) gives Fenchel equality:\n\\[\nH_\\infty(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n=\nh_c\\rho-Q_\\infty(\\beta_c,h_c).\n\\]\nThe full inequality at arbitrary \\((\\beta,h)\\in\\Omega\\) rearranges to\n\\[\nh\\rho-Q_\\infty(\\beta,h)\n\\le\nH_\\infty(\\beta_c,\\rho)-s(\\beta-\\beta_c).\n\\]\nTaking the supremum over \\(h\\) yields\n\\[\nH_\\infty(\\beta,\\rho)\n\\le\nH_\\infty(\\beta_c,\\rho)-s(\\beta-\\beta_c),\n\\qquad\\beta&gt;0. \\tag{6.10}\n\\]\nThus \\(-s\\) is a supergradient of the finite concave function \\(H_\\infty(\\cdot,\\rho)\\).</p><p>Every supergradient at an interior point lies between the right and left derivatives. Applying this to \\(w_-\\) and \\(w_+\\) gives\n\\[\nD_\\beta^-H_\\infty(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n-\nD_\\beta^+H_\\infty(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n\\ge\\gamma&gt;0. \\tag{6.11}\n\\]\nBoth derivatives are finite because \\(\\beta_c\\) is interior to the interval of finiteness of this concave function.</p><p>By Proposition \\(3\\),\n\\[\nf_\\Phi(\\beta,\\rho)=\\frac t\\beta H_\\infty(\\beta,\\rho).\n\\]\nContinuity and the one-sided product rule give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_\\beta^-f_\\Phi(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n-\nD_\\beta^+f_\\Phi(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n&amp;=\n\\frac t{\\beta_c}\n\\left(\nD_\\beta^-H_\\infty(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n-\nD_\\beta^+H_\\infty(\\beta_c,\\rho)\n\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge\\frac{t\\gamma}{\\beta_c}&gt;0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>The all-sequence canonical limit applies to \\(N_L=\\lfloor\\rho L^3\\rfloor\\) for every \\(\\beta&gt;0,\\rho&gt;0\\). The location\n\\[\n\\lambda_c\\in B((1,-3/4),r_0/8),\n\\qquad r_0=1/4,\n\\]\ngives \\(\\beta_c\\in(1/2,3/2)\\). Since \\(\\rho\\in I\\) was arbitrary, the same potential and the same critical inverse temperature work throughout \\(I\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 1", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Elementary convex facts (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Elementary convex facts (paper: Appendix A).**</p><p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">If \\(F\\) is finite and convex on an open convex set \\(O\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\), its subdifferential at \\(x\\in O\\) is\n  \\[\n  \\partial F(x)\n  =\\{v:F(y)\\ge F(x)+v\\cdot(y-x)\\text{ for every }y\\in O\\}.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a nonempty compact convex set \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\), its support function is\n  \\[\n  h_K(\\xi)=\\max_{v\\in K}\\xi\\cdot v.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite convex function \\(F\\) on an open convex subset of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\), let\n  \\[\n  G_F(x)\n  =\n  \\left\\{\n  \\lim_{j\\to\\infty}\\nabla F(x_j):\n  x_j\\to x,\\ F\\text{ is differentiable at }x_j\n  \\right\\}.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement. The local convex convergence, one-dimensional conjugacy, and directional and reachable-gradient conclusions of Lemmas \\(1.1\\)–\\(1.3\\) hold.</p>", "proof": "<p>The three assertions are established in Lemmas \\(1.1\\), \\(1.2\\), and \\(1.3\\), respectively.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Local convex convergence (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Local convex convergence (paper: Lemma A.1).**</p><p>Assumptions. Let \\(O\\subset\\mathbb R^m\\) be open and convex.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p class=\"li num\">1. Every finite convex function \\(F\\) on \\(O\\) is locally Lipschitz and has a nonempty compact subdifferential at every point. Its subgradients are bounded on compact subsets.</p><p class=\"li num\">2. If finite convex functions \\(F_n\\) converge pointwise on \\(O\\) to a finite function \\(F\\), then \\(F\\) is convex and the convergence is locally uniform.</p><p class=\"li num\">3. If \\(x_n\\to x\\in O\\), \\(F_n\\to F\\) locally uniformly, and \\(v_n\\in\\partial F_n(x_n)\\), then \\((v_n)\\) is bounded and every limit belongs to \\(\\partial F(x)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Choose a cube with center \\(c\\) whose concentric enlargement lies in \\(O\\). Convexity bounds a function above on the larger cube by its values at the vertices. The inequality\n\\[\nF(y)\\ge2F(c)-F(2c-y)\n\\]\nthen bounds it below on a smaller concentric cube. On a still smaller cube, extend any chord by a fixed positive distance in both directions while remaining in the bounded region. Monotonicity of one-dimensional convex secant slopes bounds the difference quotient on the original chord by the surrounding oscillation divided by that distance. This proves local Lipschitz continuity.</p><p>For the sequence assertion, the finitely many vertex values and the center value are uniformly bounded by pointwise convergence. The same argument gives a common local Lipschitz constant. A finite net of points on each compact set upgrades pointwise to uniform convergence. Passing convexity inequalities to limits proves convexity of \\(F\\).</p><p>For a supporting plane, separate a point strictly below the epigraph from that convex epigraph, let the point tend to \\((x,F(x))\\), and normalize the separating normals. Separation in finite dimensions follows by projecting an exterior point onto a closed convex set: if \\(z\\) is its nearest point, expansion of the squared distance along \\(z+\\theta(y-z)\\) gives the separating inequality. One can restrict the epigraph to a closed neighborhood of \\(x\\) before applying this construction.</p><p>A limiting normal has a nonzero vertical component: a horizontal supporting normal would separate \\(x\\) from an open neighborhood of \\(x\\), which is impossible. Division by that component produces a local affine minorant through \\((x,F(x))\\). Convexity extends it to all of \\(O\\) by considering the initial portion of the segment from \\(x\\) to any other point. Thus \\(\\partial F(x)\\ne\\varnothing\\).</p><p>If \\(v\\in\\partial F(x)\\) and \\(x\\pm re_j\\in O\\), the supporting inequalities bound \\(|v\\cdot e_j|\\) by the oscillation of \\(F\\) divided by \\(r\\). Uniform oscillation on a surrounding compact set bounds all subgradients on a smaller compact set. Subdifferentials are closed by their defining inequalities, hence compact.</p><p>The same bounds apply to \\(F_n\\) under local uniform convergence. Passing\n\\[\nF_n(y)\\ge F_n(x_n)+v_n\\cdot(y-x_n)\n\\]\nto a convergent subsequence proves the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.2", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**One-dimensional conjugacy (paper:", "statement": "<p>**One-dimensional conjugacy (paper: Lemma A.2).**</p><p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(u:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R\\cup\\{+\\infty\\}\\) be proper, lower semicontinuous, and convex, with domain having nonempty interior.</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  u^*(h)=\\sup_{d\\in\\mathbb R}\\{hd-u(d)\\}.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nu(d)=\\sup_{h\\in\\mathbb R}\\{hd-u^*(h)\\}.\n\\]\nAt a finite point \\(d\\), equality \\(u(d)+u^*(h)=hd\\) holds precisely when\n\\[\nu(d')\\ge u(d)+h(d'-d)\\qquad(d'\\in\\mathbb R).\n\\]</p><p>In particular, if \\(K\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\) is nonempty compact convex with a density projection of positive length, then, for every \\(x\\in\\mathbb R\\),\n\\[\n\\max\\{xs:(s,d)\\in K\\}\n=\n\\inf_{z\\in\\mathbb R}\\{h_K(x,z)-zd\\},\n\\]\nwhere the maximum is \\(-\\infty\\) outside that projection.</p>", "proof": "<p>At every interior point of the domain, a slope between the left and right convex derivatives gives an affine minorant. Hence \\(u\\) has at least one affine minorant \\(\\ell_0\\).</p><p>Fix \\((d,a)\\) strictly below the closed epigraph of \\(u\\). Strict separation, by the projection argument in Lemma \\(1.1\\), yields\n\\[\nAd'+By\\ge c&gt;Ad+Ba\n\\qquad(y\\ge u(d')).\n\\]\nNecessarily \\(B\\ge0\\). If \\(B&gt;0\\), division gives an affine minorant of \\(u\\) whose value at \\(d\\) exceeds \\(a\\). If \\(B=0\\), add a sufficiently small positive multiple of the valid inequality \\(y\\ge\\ell_0(d')\\). The resulting inequality has positive vertical coefficient and still strictly separates \\((d,a)\\). It also gives an affine minorant exceeding \\(a\\) at \\(d\\).</p><p>Thus \\(u\\) is the supremum of its affine minorants, including at boundary and exterior points of its domain. Among affine minorants of slope \\(h\\), the largest is\n\\[\nd\\longmapsto hd-u^*(h).\n\\]\nThis proves biconjugacy. Rearranging the definition of \\(u^*\\) proves the equality criterion.</p><p>For the last assertion, put\n\\[\nk_x(d)=\\max\\{xs:(s,d)\\in K\\}.\n\\]\nCompactness and convexity of \\(K\\) make \\(k_x\\) upper semicontinuous and concave on its compact projection interval. Thus \\(-k_x\\), extended by \\(+\\infty\\), satisfies the preceding hypotheses. The identity\n\\[\nh_K(x,z)=\\sup_d\\{k_x(d)+zd\\}\n\\]\nand biconjugacy give the formula.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 1.3", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Directional and reachable gradients (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Directional and reachable gradients (paper: Lemma A.3).**</p><p>Assumptions. Let \\(F\\) be finite and convex on an open convex set \\(O\\subset\\mathbb R^2\\).</p><p>Statement. For \\(x\\in O\\), \\(G_F(x)\\) is nonempty compact and\n\\[\n\\partial F(x)=\\operatorname{conv}G_F(x).\n\\]\nFor every \\(e\\in\\mathbb R^2\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{a\\downarrow0}\n\\frac{F(x+ae)-F(x)}a\n=\n\\max_{v\\in\\partial F(x)}v\\cdot e.\n\\]\nThe rescaled functions converge uniformly for \\(e\\) in compact subsets of \\(\\mathbb R^2\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>On every coordinate line, a one-dimensional convex function has equal left and right derivatives except at countably many points: both derivatives are monotone, and each strict gap contains a distinct rational number. Fubini’s theorem therefore implies that both coordinate partial derivatives of \\(F\\) exist almost everywhere.</p><p>At such a point, every subgradient has those two coordinates, so the subdifferential is a singleton, say \\(\\{v\\}\\). This implies differentiability. Indeed, for small \\(u\\), choose \\(v_u\\in\\partial F(x+u)\\). Local boundedness and closedness of subgradients show \\(v_u\\to v\\), and the two supporting inequalities give\n\\[\nv\\cdot u\\le F(x+u)-F(x)\\le v_u\\cdot u.\n\\]\nTheir difference is \\(o(|u|)\\). Thus differentiability points have full measure and are dense.</p><p>Local boundedness and diagonal subsequences show that \\(G_F(x)\\) is nonempty compact. Each element belongs to \\(\\partial F(x)\\) by the limiting supporting inequality.</p><p>Conversely, take \\(p\\in\\partial F(x)\\) and a direction \\(e\\). Choose differentiability points\n\\[\ny_j=x+a_je+o(a_j),\\qquad a_j\\downarrow0,\n\\]\nwith, for example, error less than \\(a_j^2\\). Adding the supporting inequalities at \\(x\\) and \\(y_j\\) gives\n\\[\n(\\nabla F(y_j)-p)\\cdot(y_j-x)\\ge0.\n\\]\nAfter passing to a bounded-gradient subsequence and dividing by \\(a_j\\), one obtains \\(v\\in G_F(x)\\) with \\(v\\cdot e\\ge p\\cdot e\\). Thus \\(G_F(x)\\) and \\(\\partial F(x)\\) have the same support function.</p><p>In the plane, the convex hull of a compact set is compact: any convex combination can be reduced to three terms by eliminating an affine dependence among four or more points. Separation therefore gives\n\\[\n\\partial F(x)=\\operatorname{conv}G_F(x).\n\\]</p><p>Finally, the convex secant slope\n\\[\n\\frac{F(x+ae)-F(x)}a\n\\]\nhas a limit as \\(a\\downarrow0\\). Supporting inequalities at \\(x\\) give the lower bound \\(v\\cdot e\\) for every \\(v\\in\\partial F(x)\\). Choose \\(v_a\\in\\partial F(x+ae)\\); the reverse supporting inequality bounds the same secant slope above by \\(v_a\\cdot e\\). A bounded subsequence of \\(v_a\\) converges to an element of \\(\\partial F(x)\\), proving the equality.</p><p>The local Lipschitz constant for \\(F\\) gives a common Lipschitz constant in \\(e\\) for all sufficiently small rescalings on a fixed compact set. A finite-net argument proves uniform convergence there.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 2", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Packing, the shell core, and uniform stability (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Packing, the shell core, and uniform stability (paper: Section 2.1 and Proposition 2.2).**</p><p>Definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(M_L\\) be the largest cardinality of a subset of \\(\\Lambda_L\\) whose distinct points have distance at least one. The limit\n  \\[\n  p=\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{M_L}{L^3}\\in(0,\\infty)\n  \\]\n  exists. Put\n  \\[\n  A=\\frac3{2p},\\qquad \\delta=10^{-3},\\qquad b=\\frac14.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(t&gt;1\\), put \\(E=t^2\\) and \\(e_t=e^{-t}\\), and define\n  \\[\n  \\psi_t(x)=\n  \\begin{cases}\n  +\\infty,&amp;|x|=0,\\\\\n  E+(e_t/|x|)^{12},&amp;0&lt;|x|&lt;e_t,\\\\\n  0,&amp;e_t\\le |x|\\le(1+\\delta)e_t,\\\\\n  E,&amp;(1+\\delta)e_t&lt;|x|&lt;1,\\\\\n  0,&amp;|x|\\ge1.\n  \\end{cases}\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose a nonnegative radial \\(w\\in C^1(\\mathbb R^3)\\), supported in the unit ball, strictly positive on the closed ball of radius \\(1/2\\), and normalized by\n  \\[\n  \\int_{\\mathbb R^3}w(x)\\,dx=1.\n  \\]\n  For \\(R\\ge1\\), let \\(w_R(x)=R^{-3}w(x/R)\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) be the class of potentials\n  \\[\n  \\phi=\\psi_t-S,\\qquad\n  S=t\\sum_j\\sigma_jw_{R_j},\n  \\qquad R_j\\ge1,\\quad \\sigma_j\\ge0,\\quad\n  \\sum_j\\sigma_j\\le A+1,\n  \\]\n  allowing empty, finite, or countably infinite sums.</p><p class=\"li\">Partition \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) into half-open cubes \\(C_i\\), \\(i\\in\\mathbb Z^3\\), of side \\(b\\). Let \\(n_i\\) be the number of particles in \\(C_i\\). For cropped cells in a finite cube, use the same notation. Set\n  \\[\n  J_{ij}=\\sup_{x\\in C_i,\\ y\\in C_j}S(x-y).\n  \\]\n  There is a constant \\(c_b\\), depending only on \\(b,w\\), such that\n  \\[\n  \\sum_jJ_{ij}\\le M,\\qquad M=c_bt(A+1).\n  \\]\n  Put \\(A_0=E/8\\) and \\(B_0=E/2\\).</p><p>Statement. Every \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell&lt;k}\\psi_t(x_\\ell-x_k)\n\\ge A_0\\sum_i n_i^2-B_0N,\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nU_N^\\phi\n\\ge (A_0-M/2)\\sum_i n_i^2-B_0N. \\tag{2.5}\n\\]\nThese inequalities remain valid if positive core interactions between members of a partition into unions of whole cells are deleted, or if negative interactions are multiplied by factors in \\([0,1]\\).</p><p>All sufficiently large \\(t\\) satisfy\n\\[\n2(1+\\delta)e_t&lt;1,\\qquad\nA_0\\ge8M,\\qquad\n\\kappa:=(A_0-M/2)b^3&gt;10t(A+1). \\tag{2.6}\n\\]\nFor such \\(t\\), every \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\) is stable with constant \\(B_0\\), finite and locally bounded away from the origin, divergent to \\(+\\infty\\) at the origin, nonpositive for \\(|x|\\ge1\\), and integrable outside the unit ball. If some \\(\\sigma_j&gt;0\\) has \\(R_j&gt;4\\), then \\(\\phi\\) is uniformly strictly negative on \\(1\\le|x|\\le2\\), and hence is admissible.</p>", "proof": "<p>The packing number is finite, and compactness gives a maximizing configuration. Disjoint balls of radius \\(1/2\\) around packing points lie in the cube of side \\(L+1\\), giving an upper bound on \\(M_L/L^3\\) for \\(L\\ge1\\). A unit lattice gives a positive lower bound.</p><p>Repeat a maximizing packing in a cube of side \\(s\\) with period \\(s+1\\) in each coordinate. Points in distinct copies remain separated by at least one, so\n\\[\n\\liminf_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{M_L}{L^3}\n\\ge\\frac{M_s}{(s+1)^3}.\n\\]\nLet \\(s\\to\\infty\\) along a sequence realizing the limsup. This proves existence of \\(p\\in(0,\\infty)\\).</p><p>Since\n\\[\n\\|w_R\\|_\\infty\\le\\|w\\|_\\infty,\\qquad \\|w_R\\|_1=1,\n\\]\nthe attractive series converges uniformly and in \\(L^1\\) in the infinite case. Thus \\(S\\) is bounded, continuous, and nonnegative.</p><p>For one kernel \\(w_R\\), each nonzero cell supremum is at most \\(\\|w\\|_\\infty R^{-3}\\), and at most \\(C_bR^3\\) cells are within distance \\(R\\) of a given cell, because \\(R\\ge1\\). Summing over coefficients \\(t\\sigma_j\\) proves\n\\[\n\\sum_jJ_{ij}\\le c_bt(A+1).\n\\]\nTaking a supremum of a sum only decreases the result relative to the sum of suprema. The argument therefore also applies to infinite sums and cropped cells.</p><p>The diameter of a cell is \\(\\sqrt3/4&lt;1\\). By Lemma \\(2.1\\), among the \\(n_i(n_i-1)/2\\) pairs in cell \\(i\\), at most \\(3n_i^2/8\\) lie in the shell. Every other pair has core energy at least \\(E\\). Thus the core energy inside that cell is at least\n\\[\nE\\left(\\frac{n_i(n_i-1)}2-\\frac{3n_i^2}8\\right)\n=A_0n_i^2-B_0n_i.\n\\]\nAll remaining core interactions are nonnegative. Also,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell&lt;k}S(x_\\ell-x_k)\n\\le\\frac12\\sum_{i,j}J_{ij}n_in_j\n\\le\\frac M2\\sum_i n_i^2,\n\\]\nwhere the first bound includes harmless self-particle terms, and the second uses symmetry and\n\\[\n2n_in_j\\le n_i^2+n_j^2.\n\\]\nThis proves (2.5). The permitted deletions preserve every core interaction within a small cell. Reducing a negative term only increases the energy, so the same proof applies.</p><p>The conditions (2.6) hold for all sufficiently large \\(t\\), since \\(A_0\\) is quadratic in \\(t\\) and \\(M\\) is linear in \\(t\\). They imply stability with constant \\(B_0\\).</p><p>The remaining regularity properties follow from the definition of \\(\\psi_t\\) and the boundedness and \\(L^1\\) convergence of \\(S\\). If \\(\\sigma_j&gt;0\\) and \\(R_j&gt;4\\), the corresponding rescaled kernel has a positive minimum on \\(1\\le|x|\\le2\\), where the core vanishes. This supplies the required attractive interval.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 2.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**The shell graph (paper:", "statement": "<p>**The shell graph (paper: Lemma 2.1).**</p><p>Assumptions. Use \\(\\delta=10^{-3}\\) and \\(e_t=e^{-t}\\) from Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p>Statement. No five points in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\) have all mutual distances in\n\\[\n[e_t,(1+\\delta)e_t].\n\\]\nConsequently, a graph whose vertices are points of \\(\\mathbb R^3\\), with edges joining pairs at those distances, has at most \\(3n^2/8\\) edges on any set of \\(n\\) vertices.</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose five such points existed. Scale by \\(e_t^{-1}\\) and form the Gram matrix \\(G\\) of the four vectors from one point to the other four. Write\n\\[\n\\varepsilon=2\\delta+\\delta^2.\n\\]\nThe diagonal entries differ from \\(1\\) by at most \\(\\varepsilon\\). By polarization, each off-diagonal entry differs from \\(1/2\\) by at most \\(\\varepsilon\\).</p><p>The matrix \\(G_0\\) with diagonal \\(1\\) and off-diagonal \\(1/2\\) has smallest eigenvalue \\(1/2\\), whereas\n\\[\n\\|G-G_0\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}\n\\le4\\varepsilon=0.008004&lt;\\frac12.\n\\]\nThus \\(G\\) would have rank four, impossible for four vectors in \\(\\mathbb R^3\\).</p><p>For the graph bound, maximize\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\text{ an edge}}a_ia_j\n\\quad\\text{over}\\quad\na_i\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_i a_i=1.\n\\]\nChoose a maximizer with smallest support. If two vertices in its support are nonadjacent, transfer all their combined weight to the one with the larger weighted neighbor sum. This cannot decrease the objective and decreases the support, a contradiction.</p><p>The support is therefore a clique of at most four vertices. At this maximizer,\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\text{ an edge}}a_ia_j\n=\\frac12\\left(1-\\sum_i a_i^2\\right)\\le\\frac38.\n\\]\nApplying this to \\(a_i=1/n\\) gives the asserted edge count. The case \\(n=0\\) is immediate.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 3", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Canonical and grand-canonical limits (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Canonical and grand-canonical limits (paper: Proposition 2.3).**</p><p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t\\) satisfying (2.6) and \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\), with the core, cells, and constants of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Write\n  \\[\n  Z_{L,n}(\\beta)=Z_{\\Lambda_L,n}^\\phi(\\beta),\\qquad\n  a_L(\\beta,n)=-\\frac{\\log Z_{L,n}(\\beta)}{tL^3},\n  \\]\n  with \\(Z_{L,0}=1\\) and \\(0\\log0=0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\Omega=(0,\\infty)\\times\\mathbb R\\), with coordinates \\((\\beta,h)\\), and define\n  \\[\n  \\Xi_L^\\phi(\\beta,h)=\\sum_{n\\ge0}e^{thn}Z_{L,n}(\\beta).\n  \\]</p><p>Statement. For every \\(\\beta&gt;0\\) and \\(d\\ge0\\), there is a finite all-sequence limit\n\\[\nH_\\phi(\\beta,d)\n=-\\lim_{\\substack{L\\to\\infty\\\\ n/L^3\\to d}}\n\\frac{\\log Z_{L,n}(\\beta)}{tL^3},\n\\qquad H_\\phi(\\beta,0)=0.\n\\]\nExtend \\(H_\\phi(\\beta,d)\\) by \\(+\\infty\\) for \\(d&lt;0\\). It is closed and convex in \\(d\\), and finite and concave in \\(\\beta\\) for every \\(d\\ge0\\). Moreover,\n\\[\nH_\\phi(\\beta,d)\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\beta\\kappa}{t}d^2\n-\\frac{\\beta B_0}{t}d\n+\\frac{d\\log d-d}{t}. \\tag{2.8}\n\\]</p><p>The finite convex pressure is\n\\[\nQ_\\phi(\\beta,h)\n=\n\\lim_{L\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\log\\Xi_L^\\phi(\\beta,h)}{tL^3}\n=\n\\sup_{d\\ge0}\\{hd-H_\\phi(\\beta,d)\\}. \\tag{2.9}\n\\]\nThe convergence is uniform on compact subsets of \\(\\Omega\\), and\n\\[\nH_\\phi(\\beta,d)\n=\n\\sup_{h\\in\\mathbb R}\\{hd-Q_\\phi(\\beta,h)\\},\n\\qquad d\\in\\mathbb R. \\tag{2.10}\n\\]\nThe constants in these bounds are common to \\(\\mathcal C_t\\). In particular,\n\\[\nf_\\phi(\\beta,d)=\\frac t\\beta H_\\phi(\\beta,d)\n\\qquad(\\beta&gt;0,\\ d&gt;0). \\tag{2.16}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>First establish two finite-volume bounds.</p><p>Fix \\(D_0&lt;\\infty\\), and choose a grid spacing \\(q\\in(0,1/2)\\) with \\(q^{-3}&gt;D_0+1\\). For large \\(L\\), at least \\(D_0L^3\\) grid centers lie in \\(\\Lambda_L\\), at distance \\(q/8\\) from its boundary. For \\(n\\le D_0L^3\\), put one particle in each of \\(n\\) distinct balls of radius \\(q/8\\) around those centers.</p><p>Every pair has distance at least \\(3q/4\\), and each particle has at most \\(Cq^{-3}\\) neighbors at distance less than one. The core is bounded on \\([3q/4,1]\\), so the positive energy is at most \\(E_qn\\) for some finite \\(E_q\\). Negative energy need not be counted. The \\(n!\\) assignments of labeled particles to balls have disjoint integration regions and cancel the canonical factorial. If \\(v_q\\) is the ball volume,\n\\[\nZ_{L,n}(\\beta)\\ge v_q^ne^{-\\beta E_qn}.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\na_L(\\beta,n)\\le C_{D_0,\\beta}\n\\qquad(0\\le n\\le D_0L^3,\\ L\\text{ sufficiently large}). \\tag{2.11}\n\\]\nThe constant is uniform over \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) and locally uniform in \\(\\beta&gt;0\\).</p><p>There are at most \\((L/b+2)^3\\) cropped cells. Cauchy–Schwarz in (2.5), followed by \\(n!\\ge(n/e)^n\\), gives, with \\(d_L=n/L^3\\),\n\\[\na_L(\\beta,n)\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\beta\\kappa}{t(1+2b/L)^3}d_L^2\n-\\frac{\\beta B_0}{t}d_L\n+\\frac{d_L\\log d_L-d_L}{t}. \\tag{2.12}\n\\]\nDropping the positive quadratic term gives the corresponding linear lower bound. Thus \\(Z_{L,n}\\) is positive and finite, and \\(a_L\\) is bounded above and below on bounded density intervals for large \\(L\\).</p><p>Fix \\(\\beta&gt;0\\) and \\(d&gt;0\\). The increasing infima\n\\[\nI_j\n=\n\\inf\\left\\{\na_L(\\beta,n):\nL\\ge j,\\ \\left|\\frac n{L^3}-d\\right|\\le\\frac1j\n\\right\\}\n\\]\nconverge to a finite number \\(I\\). Choose \\(\\ell_j\\ge j\\) and \\(n_j\\) in the defining set with\n\\[\na_{\\ell_j}(\\beta,n_j)\\le I_j+\\frac1j.\n\\]\nThen \\(n_j/\\ell_j^3\\to d\\) and \\(a_{\\ell_j}(\\beta,n_j)\\to I\\). Every sequence with \\(L\\to\\infty\\) and \\(n/L^3\\to d\\) has liminf at least \\(I_j\\) for each \\(j\\), and hence at least \\(I\\).</p><p>For the reverse inequality, tile part of a large cube with\n\\[\nk=\\left\\lfloor\\frac L{\\ell_j+2}\\right\\rfloor^3\n\\]\ncubes of side \\(\\ell_j\\), leaving gaps of width two. All interactions between distinct cubes are nonpositive. For prescribed counts \\(m_1,\\ldots,m_k\\),\n\\[\nZ_{L,\\sum_i m_i}(\\beta)\n\\ge\\prod_{i=1}^k Z_{\\ell_j,m_i}(\\beta). \\tag{2.13}\n\\]\nIndeed, the \\((\\sum_i m_i)!/\\prod_i m_i!\\) assignments of labels to cubes give exactly the factorials on the right.</p><p>Start with \\(n_j\\) particles in every small cube. Put\n\\[\nn_{\\mathrm{hi}}=\\lfloor(4d+4)\\ell_j^3\\rfloor,\\qquad\nd_b=\\frac{n_j}{(\\ell_j+2)^3},\\qquad\nd_{\\mathrm{hi}}=\\frac{n_{\\mathrm{hi}}}{(\\ell_j+2)^3}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(j\\), \\(n_j&gt;0\\), \\(n_{\\mathrm{hi}}&gt;n_j\\), \\(d_b\\to d\\), and \\(d_{\\mathrm{hi}}\\to4d+4\\).</p><p>If a target count \\(n\\) exceeds \\(kn_j\\), change some cubes to \\(n_{\\mathrm{hi}}\\); if it is smaller, empty some cubes. One additional intermediate count realizes \\(n\\) exactly. For fixed sufficiently large \\(j\\), this is possible for all large \\(L\\) with \\(n/L^3\\to d\\). The limiting upper fraction of changed cubes is at most\n\\[\nc_j\n=\n\\max\\left\\{\n\\frac{(d-d_b)_+}{d_{\\mathrm{hi}}-d_b},\n\\frac{(d_b-d)_+}{d_b}\n\\right\\},\n\\qquad c_j\\to0.\n\\]\nAll new counts are at most \\((4d+4)\\ell_j^3\\), so (2.11) bounds their \\(a_{\\ell_j}\\) uniformly above. The baseline values are bounded in absolute value. Applying (2.13), taking \\(L\\to\\infty\\) with \\(j\\) fixed, and then \\(j\\to\\infty\\), gives\n\\[\n\\limsup_{\\substack{L\\to\\infty\\\\ n/L^3\\to d}}a_L(\\beta,n)\n\\le\n\\lim_{j\\to\\infty}\n\\left(\\frac{\\ell_j}{\\ell_j+2}\\right)^3\n\\bigl(a_{\\ell_j}(\\beta,n_j)+Cc_j\\bigr)\n=I.\n\\]\nThe single intermediate cube contributes zero in the first limit. This proves all-sequence convergence for \\(d&gt;0\\).</p><p>When \\(n/L^3\\to0\\), the linear lower bound has limit zero. For an upper bound, fix a sufficiently large side \\(\\ell\\) and use separated cubes as in (2.13), with empty baseline. Fill some cubes with \\(\\lfloor\\ell^3\\rfloor\\) particles and at most one with an intermediate count. Their number is \\(o(L^3)\\), so (2.11) gives limiting upper bound zero. Hence \\(H_\\phi(\\beta,0)=0\\).</p><p>For convexity, take \\(0\\le d_1&lt;d_2\\) and a density strictly between them. For large fixed \\(\\ell\\), use counts \\(\\lfloor d_1\\ell^3\\rfloor\\) and \\(\\lfloor d_2\\ell^3\\rfloor\\) in the separated cubes. The target density lies between their diluted densities for all sufficiently large \\(\\ell\\). Choose proportions attaining the target count, with at most one intermediate cube. Apply (2.13), first let \\(L\\to\\infty\\), and then \\(\\ell\\to\\infty\\). This gives convexity of \\(H_\\phi(\\beta,\\cdot)\\).</p><p>The function is finite and convex on \\([0,\\infty)\\), hence continuous on \\((0,\\infty)\\). The linear lower bound gives lower semicontinuity at zero, so extension by \\(+\\infty\\) to negative densities is closed and convex. Hölder’s inequality makes \\(-\\log Z_{L,n}(\\beta)\\) concave in \\(\\beta\\). Passage to the limit gives finite concavity of \\(H_\\phi(\\cdot,d)\\). Equation (2.12) gives (2.8).</p><p>For the pressure, on a compact subset of \\(\\Omega\\), (2.12) implies that for sufficiently large \\(D\\) and all large \\(L\\),\n\\[\ne^{thn}Z_{L,n}(\\beta)\\le e^{-cn^2/L^3}\n\\qquad(n&gt;DL^3),\n\\]\nwith \\(c&gt;0\\) independent of \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\). Their sum is exponentially small in \\(L^3\\), while the empty term is one.</p><p>Among the remaining at most \\(\\lfloor DL^3\\rfloor+1\\) terms, choose one of maximal size. Every subsequence has a further subsequence whose density converges to some \\(d\\in[0,D]\\). The all-sequence canonical limit identifies its limiting exponent as \\(hd-H_\\phi(\\beta,d)\\). Conversely, the term \\(n=\\lfloor dL^3\\rfloor\\) supplies that exponent as a lower bound for every \\(d\\ge0\\). This proves (2.9).</p><p>Finite-volume logarithms are finite and jointly convex in \\((\\beta,h)\\), by Hölder’s inequality. The limit is finite by the density bound and nonnegative because the empty configuration is allowed. Proposition \\(1\\) upgrades pointwise convergence to local uniform convergence.</p><p>Finally, the closed convexity in density permits the biconjugacy identity of Proposition \\(1\\), giving (2.10). The normalization of \\(H_\\phi\\) immediately yields (2.16).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 4", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The quadratic density transform (paper:", "statement": "<p>**The quadratic density transform (paper: Proposition 2.4).**</p><p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t\\) satisfying (2.6), and let \\(Q=Q_\\phi\\) for \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Define\n  \\[\n  H_Q(\\beta,d)=\\sup_{h\\in\\mathbb R}\\{hd-Q(\\beta,h)\\}.\n  \\]\n  By Proposition \\(3\\), this equals \\(H_\\phi\\), including the value \\(+\\infty\\) for \\(d&lt;0\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(0\\le\\sigma\\le A+1\\), define\n  \\[\n  (T_\\sigma Q)(\\beta,h)\n  =\n  \\sup_{d\\ge0}\n  \\left\\{\n  hd-H_Q(\\beta,d)+\\frac{\\beta\\sigma}{2}d^2\n  \\right\\}. \\tag{2.14}\n  \\]</p><p>Statement. \\(T_\\sigma Q\\) is finite and convex on \\(\\Omega\\), nonnegative, and nondecreasing in \\(h\\). The supremum is attained. For every compact \\(W\\subset\\Omega\\), there is \\(D_W&lt;\\infty\\), depending only on \\(W,t,b,w,A\\), such that every maximizing density lies in \\([0,D_W]\\), uniformly in \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\) and \\(0\\le\\sigma\\le A+1\\). The transformed pressures are uniformly bounded on \\(W\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>The value at \\(d=0\\) is zero. By (2.8), the objective is bounded above by\n\\[\nhd-\\beta\\left(\\frac\\kappa t-\\frac\\sigma2\\right)d^2\n+\\frac{\\beta B_0}t d\n-\\frac{d\\log d-d}{t}. \\tag{2.15}\n\\]\nCondition (2.6) gives a uniform positive lower bound on \\(\\kappa/t-\\sigma/2\\). On a compact parameter set with \\(\\beta\\) bounded away from zero, this expression is negative for every sufficiently large \\(d\\), uniformly in the potential and \\(\\sigma\\). This proves the common density bound and the finite upper bound.</p><p>The objective is upper semicontinuous in \\(d\\), since \\(H_Q(\\beta,\\cdot)\\) is lower semicontinuous, so it attains its maximum on the resulting compact interval.</p><p>For fixed \\(d\\ge0\\), the objective is convex in \\((\\beta,h)\\): the canonical function is concave in \\(\\beta\\), and the other terms are affine. Its supremum is therefore convex. Monotonicity in \\(h\\) follows from \\(d\\ge0\\), and nonnegativity follows from the zero-density choice.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 5", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Uniform local moments (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Uniform local moments (paper: Proposition 3.1).**</p><p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t\\) satisfying (2.6) and the class \\(\\mathcal C_t\\) of Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In a finite cube, use the grand-canonical measure with inverse temperature \\(\\beta&gt;0\\) and activity \\(e^{th}\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Allow positive core interactions to be deleted between boxes that are unions of whole \\(b\\)-cells, and negative pair interactions to be multiplied by measurable symmetric factors in \\([0,1]\\). Every original cell retains its internal core interaction.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(n_i\\) denote cell occupations.</p><p>Statement. For every compact \\(K\\subset\\Omega\\), there is \\(C_K&lt;\\infty\\) such that, for all these potentials, finite cubes, and modified systems,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_{\\beta,h}[n_in_j]\\le C_K\n\\qquad((\\beta,h)\\in K)\n\\]\nfor every pair of cells meeting the cube. The constant is independent of the number and ranges of attractive summands.</p>", "proof": "<p>Condition on the configuration \\(Y\\) outside \\(C_i\\), and put\n\\[\nA_i(Y)=\\sum_{j\\ne i}J_{ij}n_j,\\qquad\nc=\\frac{\\beta A_0}{4}.\n\\]\nInteractions involving the \\(n\\) particles inside \\(C_i\\) are bounded below by\n\\[\n(A_0-J_{ii}/2)n^2-B_0n-nA_i.\n\\]\nThe empty cell contributes one to the conditional partition function, and its volume is at most \\(b^3\\), including cropped cells. Hence\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E[e^{cn_i^2}\\mid Y]\n&amp;\\le\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\frac{b^{3n}}{n!}\n\\exp\\!\\left(\ncn^2+thn-\\beta(A_0-J_{ii}/2)n^2\n+\\beta B_0n+\\beta nA_i\n\\right)\\\\\n&amp;\\le C_1\\exp(\\beta A_i^2/A_0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nIndeed,\n\\[\n\\beta nA_i\\le\\frac{\\beta A_0}4n^2+\\frac{\\beta A_i^2}{A_0};\n\\]\nafter this estimate, the remaining negative quadratic coefficient is at least\n\\[\n\\beta(A_0/2-M/2)\\ge\\frac{7\\beta A_0}{16}.\n\\]\nThe resulting one-variable series is bounded uniformly on \\(K\\).</p><p>Let \\(F_i=\\mathbb E[e^{cn_i^2}]\\). These numbers are finite before the bootstrap: multiplying the grand-canonical integrand by \\(e^{cn_i^2}\\) leaves a strictly negative quadratic in all cell counts, by (2.5). Weighted Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\\[\nA_i^2\\le M\\sum_{j\\ne i}J_{ij}n_j^2.\n\\]\nIf \\(M&gt;0\\), put\n\\[\n\\theta_{ij}=J_{ij}/M,\\qquad\n\\alpha=4(M/A_0)^2&lt;1.\n\\]\nThen \\(c\\alpha=\\beta M^2/A_0\\). The row weights sum to at most one; place missing weight on a value zero. Convexity of the exponential and then concavity of \\(x^\\alpha\\) give\n\\[\nF_i\\le C_1\\left(1+\\sup_jF_j^\\alpha\\right).\n\\]\nThere are finitely many cells, so their maximum is bounded by a constant depending only on \\(C_1,\\alpha\\). If \\(M=0\\), the conditional estimate already gives \\(F_i\\le C_1\\).</p><p>Since \\(\\beta\\) is bounded away from zero on \\(K\\), this proves uniform second moments. A final Cauchy–Schwarz inequality proves the assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 6", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Attractive perturbations and tail continuity (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Attractive perturbations and tail continuity (paper: Proposition 3.2).**</p><p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use the fixed-\\(t\\) physical class and modified finite-volume systems of Proposition \\(5\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Fix a compact \\(K\\subset\\Omega\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Consider two such systems differing by a negative interaction. Let \\(J_{ij}^{\\mathrm{diff}}\\) be cellwise suprema of its absolute value, and suppose linear interpolation stays within the same attractive mass bound.</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n|\\log\\Xi^{(1)}-\\log\\Xi^{(0)}|\n\\le\n\\frac{\\beta C_K}{2}\n\\sum_{i,j}J_{ij}^{\\mathrm{diff}}\n\\qquad((\\beta,h)\\in K). \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\nIn particular, for\n\\[\n\\phi=\\psi_t-t\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\sigma_jw_{R_j},\n\\qquad\n\\phi_n=\\psi_t-t\\sum_{j\\le n}\\sigma_jw_{R_j}\n\\]\nin \\(\\mathcal C_t\\), there is a range-independent constant \\(C_K'\\) such that\n\\[\n\\|Q_\\phi-Q_{\\phi_n}\\|_K\n\\le C_K'\\sum_{j&gt;n}\\sigma_j, \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\|F\\|_K=\\sup_{\\lambda\\in K}|F(\\lambda)|\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Differentiate the logarithm of the grand partition function along the interpolation. The absolute derivative is at most\n\\[\n\\frac\\beta2\n\\sum_{i,j}J_{ij}^{\\mathrm{diff}}\\,\n\\mathbb E[n_in_j].\n\\]\nIncluding particle self pairs only enlarges the bound. Differentiation under the integrals and sums is valid by the common negative quadratic in the finitely many cell counts. Proposition \\(5\\), followed by integration over the interpolation parameter, proves (3.1).</p><p>For the tail difference, each row sum is at most\n\\[\nc_bt\\sum_{j&gt;n}\\sigma_j,\n\\]\nand the number of cells is at most \\((L/b+2)^3\\). Divide (3.1) by \\(tL^3\\), use the pressure limit of Proposition \\(3\\), and let \\(L\\to\\infty\\). The bounds are uniform on \\(K\\), proving (3.2).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 7", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Physical Kac approximation (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Physical Kac approximation (paper: Proposition 3.3).**</p><p>Assumptions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t\\) satisfying (2.6).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  \\phi=\\psi_t-t\\sum_{j=1}^k\\sigma_jw_{R_j}\n  \\]\n  have finitely many attractive summands, with the empty sum allowed.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(\\sigma\\ge0\\) such that\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{j=1}^k\\sigma_j+\\sigma\\le A+1.\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\nQ_{\\phi-t\\sigma w_R}\\longrightarrow T_\\sigma Q_\\phi\n\\qquad(R\\to\\infty) \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\nuniformly on compact subsets of \\(\\Omega\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Fix the reference potential, and let \\(R_0\\) bound its interaction range. All constants depending on \\(R_0\\) remain fixed during this proof.</p><p>Partition a cube of side \\(L=mu\\) into \\(m^3\\) boxes of side\n\\[\nu=b\\left\\lfloor R^{1/2}/b\\right\\rfloor.\n\\]\nTranslate the \\(b\\)-cell lattice to align it with the outside cube. The \\(u\\)-boxes are unions of whole cells. For each \\(R\\), first let \\(m\\to\\infty\\), and only afterward let \\(R\\to\\infty\\). Thus \\(u\\to\\infty\\), \\(u/R\\to0\\), and eventually \\(u\\gg R_0\\).</p><p>Write \\(B_z\\) for the box centered at \\(z\\), and set\n\\[\nK_{zz'}^+\n=\\sup_{x\\in B_z,\\ y\\in B_{z'}}w_R(x-y),\n\\qquad\nK_{zz'}^-\n=\\bigl(w_R(z-z')-Cu/R^4\\bigr)_+.\n\\]\nChoose \\(C\\) large enough that \\(K_{zz'}^-\\) is at most the corresponding infimum. The \\(C^1\\) bound and compact support of \\(w\\) give \\(\\eta_R=O(u/R)\\to0\\) such that\n\\[\nu^3\\sum_{z'}K_{zz'}^+\\le1+\\eta_R, \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\nand, for \\(z\\) farther than \\(R+3u\\) from the outside boundary,\n\\[\n\\sum_{z'}K_{zz'}^-\\ge(1-\\eta_R)u^{-3}. \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\nThe sums run over the finite center grid.</p><p>To check these estimates, compare\n\\[\nu^3\\sum_{z'}w_R(z-z')\n\\]\nwith \\(\\int w_R=1\\). Oscillation in each grid cube is \\(O(uR^{-4})\\), and only \\(O((R/u)^3)\\) cubes meet the support enlarged by \\(3u\\). After multiplication by \\(u^3\\), the error is \\(O(u/R)\\). The same argument controls the suprema and truncated lower kernel. Also \\(K_{zz'}^\\pm\\le CR^{-3}\\).</p><p>For the upper bound, delete positive core interactions between \\(u\\)-boxes, increasing the partition function. Remove the reference attractive interactions between those boxes, retaining the new Kac attraction. By Proposition \\(6\\), this costs at most \\(C_{K,R_0}L^3/u\\) in the logarithm. Only original cells within \\(R_0+2b\\) of a box boundary have a nonzero difference row, and there are \\(O_{R_0}(L^3/u)\\) such cells. Proposition \\(5\\) applies throughout the interpolation.</p><p>If \\(n_z\\) is the count in \\(B_z\\), the long-range pair sum is at most\n\\[\n\\frac12\\sum_{z,z'}K_{zz'}^+n_zn_{z'}\n\\le\\frac{1+\\eta_R}{2u^3}\\sum_z n_z^2,\n\\]\nusing symmetry and \\(n_zn_{z'}\\le(n_z^2+n_{z'}^2)/2\\). The first bound includes self pairs, harmless for this upper estimate of an attractive Boltzmann factor.</p><p>The remaining reference interactions lie within individual boxes. Summing assignments of particle labels cancels the global canonical factorial, giving\n\\[\n\\Xi_{mu}^{\\phi-t\\sigma w_R}\n\\le\ne^{C_{K,R_0}L^3/u}\n\\left[\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}\ne^{thn}Z_{u,n}^\\phi(\\beta)\n\\exp\\left(\n\\frac{\\beta t\\sigma(1+\\eta_R)}{2u^3}n^2\n\\right)\n\\right]^{m^3}. \\tag{3.6}\n\\]</p><p>The bound (2.12) and \\(\\kappa&gt;10t(A+1)\\) leave the effective coefficient\n\\[\n\\frac{\\kappa}{(1+2b/u)^3}\n-\\frac{t\\sigma(1+\\eta_R)}2&gt;0\n\\]\nuniformly away from zero for large \\(R\\). Thus counts \\(n/u^3&gt;D\\), for sufficiently large \\(D\\), are suppressed by a uniform negative quadratic. Their sum is exponentially small relative to the empty term.</p><p>Among the remaining \\(O(u^3)\\) terms, every maximizing subsequence has a further subsequence with \\(n/u^3\\to d\\in[0,D]\\). The all-sequence limit of Proposition \\(3\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\frac1{tu^3}\n\\log\\left[\n\\sum_{n\\ge0}\ne^{thn}Z_{u,n}^\\phi(\\beta)\n\\exp\\left(\n\\frac{\\beta t\\sigma(1+\\eta_R)}{2u^3}n^2\n\\right)\n\\right]\n=\n\\sup_{d\\ge0}\n\\left\\{\nhd-H_\\phi(\\beta,d)+\\frac{\\beta\\sigma d^2}{2}\n\\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe logarithm of the number of low-count terms divided by \\(u^3\\) vanishes. The upper subsequence argument and the choice \\(n=\\lfloor du^3\\rfloor\\) prove the equality. Letting \\(m\\to\\infty\\) in (3.6), then \\(R\\to\\infty\\), gives the upper estimate.</p><p>For the lower bound, put a cube of side \\(s=u-2\\) at each center and require exactly \\(n=\\lfloor ds^3\\rfloor\\) particles in every smaller cube, for fixed \\(d\\ge0\\). The gaps have width two. Reference interactions between distinct smaller cubes are nonpositive and can be discarded in a lower bound.</p><p>Let \\(m_{\\mathrm{int}}\\) be the number of centers satisfying the interior condition in (3.5). Then\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\ell&lt;j}w_R(x_\\ell-x_j)\n\\ge\n\\frac12m_{\\mathrm{int}}n^2u^{-3}(1-\\eta_R)\n-\\frac12m^3nCR^{-3}.\n\\]\nThe last term removes particle self pairs included in the diagonal contribution. Count constraints and canonical factorials give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Xi_{mu}^{\\phi-t\\sigma w_R}\n\\ge\n\\bigl(e^{thn}Z_{s,n}^\\phi(\\beta)\\bigr)^{m^3}\n\\exp\\left\\{\n\\frac{\\beta t\\sigma}{2}\n\\left[\nm_{\\mathrm{int}}n^2u^{-3}(1-\\eta_R)\n-m^3nCR^{-3}\n\\right]\n\\right\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFor fixed \\(R\\), \\(m_{\\mathrm{int}}/m^3\\to1\\). Then \\(R\\to\\infty\\) gives \\(s/u\\to1\\), \\(n/s^3\\to d\\), and vanishing self-pair error. Proposition \\(3\\) yields the pressure lower bound\n\\[\nhd-H_\\phi(\\beta,d)+\\frac{\\beta\\sigma d^2}{2}.\n\\]\nTake the supremum over \\(d\\ge0\\).</p><p>This proves pointwise convergence. Both sides are finite convex functions on \\(\\Omega\\), by Propositions \\(3\\) and \\(4\\). Proposition \\(1\\) upgrades it to local uniform convergence.</p><p>The reference is fixed before the new range tends to infinity; no uniform rate over changing reference ranges is asserted.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 8", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The free-cluster reference pressure (paper:", "statement": "<p>**The free-cluster reference pressure (paper: Proposition 4.1).**</p><p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Use \\(p,\\delta,\\psi_t\\) from Proposition \\(2\\).</p><p class=\"li\">In this proposition, unlike the fixed-\\(t\\) propositions above, let \\(t\\to\\infty\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  q_t=Q_{\\psi_t},\n  \\]\n  with the normalization \\(1/(tL^3)\\) and activity \\(e^{th}\\).</p><p>Statement. Uniformly on compact subsets of \\(\\Omega\\),\n\\[\nq_t(\\beta,h)\\longrightarrow\nq_*(h):=\\max(0,ph,4ph-9p). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Call a configuration free if every pair distance below one lies in\n\\[\n[e^{-t},(1+\\delta)e^{-t}].\n\\]\nJoin points at distance below one. A two-edge path has endpoint distance at most \\(2(1+\\delta)e^{-t}&lt;1\\); freeness makes the endpoints adjacent. Thus connected components are complete graphs. Lemma \\(2.1\\), contained in Proposition \\(2\\), bounds their sizes by four. Representatives from distinct components are separated by at least one, so their number is at most \\(M_L\\).</p><p>Put \\(V=L^3\\), and let \\(\\Xi_{\\mathrm{free}}\\) be the grand configurational integral restricted to free configurations. Root each component and integrate its root over volume \\(V\\). Every other point of the component lies in a ball of volume at most \\(c_0e^{-3t}\\) about the root. Labeled block enumeration followed by division by the canonical factorial gives\n\\[\n\\Xi_{\\mathrm{free}}\n\\le\n\\sum_{k=0}^{M_L}\\frac{(V\\Theta_t)^k}{k!},\n\\qquad\n\\Theta_t\n=\n\\sum_{m=1}^4\n\\frac{e^{thm}(c_0e^{-3t})^{m-1}}{(m-1)!}. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\nIndeed, ordered component sizes give \\(1/(k!\\prod_i m_i!)\\). Allowing any of the \\(m_i\\) labels to be a root replaces \\(m_i!\\) by \\((m_i-1)!\\) and only increases the count. Ignoring root separation also enlarges the integral.</p><p>A bad edge is a pair at distance below one but outside the shell. If there are \\(B\\) bad edges, their endpoint set \\(S\\) has size at most \\(2B\\), its complement is free, and the core energy is at least \\(t^2B\\). Hence, pointwise on labeled configurations,\n\\[\ne^{-\\beta U^{\\psi_t}}\n\\le\n\\sum_{S\\subset\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}}\ne^{-\\beta t^2|S|/2}\n\\mathbf1_{\\{\\text{the complement of }S\\text{ is free}\\}}.\n\\]\nThe term corresponding to the actual bad-edge endpoint set proves this inequality; zero Boltzmann factors cause no difficulty. Summing particle numbers and integrating removed particles gives\n\\[\n\\Xi_L^{\\psi_t}\n\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left(Ve^{th-\\beta t^2/2}\\right)\n\\Xi_{\\mathrm{free}}. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]</p><p>Now\n\\[\n\\frac1t\\log\\Theta_t\n\\longrightarrow\n\\max_{1\\le m\\le4}\\{mh-3(m-1)\\}\n=\n\\max(h,4h-9),\n\\]\nuniformly for bounded \\(h\\). With \\(u=k/V\\), the factorial estimate bounds the remaining normalized logarithm in (4.2) by\n\\[\n\\frac{u(1-\\log u)}t,\n\\]\nwith value zero at \\(u=0\\). This is uniformly \\(o(1)\\) for bounded \\(u\\). There are \\(M_L+1=O(V)\\) terms. First let \\(L\\to\\infty\\), using \\(M_L/V\\to p\\). The extra pressure from (4.3) is \\(e^{th-\\beta t^2/2}/t\\), which tends to zero locally uniformly on \\(\\Omega\\). This proves the upper limit.</p><p>For the lower limit, fix \\(\\ell&gt;0\\) and \\(\\eta&gt;0\\). Dilate an optimal \\(M_\\ell\\)-packing by \\(1+\\eta\\), and repeat it with cubic period\n\\[\nP=(1+\\eta)(\\ell+2).\n\\]\nIn a cube of side \\(mP\\), the \\(k=m^3M_\\ell\\) centers have mutual distances at least \\(1+\\eta\\) and a fixed positive boundary margin.</p><p>At every center put either one particle or four particles, using the same choice \\(m_0\\in\\{1,4\\}\\). The root ranges over a ball of fixed volume \\(v_0&gt;0\\), small compared with the separation margin. For \\(m_0=4\\), put the remaining particles in relative balls of volume \\(v_1e^{-3t}\\) about the other three vertices of a regular tetrahedron of side\n\\[\n(1+\\delta/2)e^{-t}.\n\\]\nA sufficiently small fixed \\(v_1&gt;0\\) puts every internal distance in the shell. For large \\(t\\), distances between clusters exceed one.</p><p>All these configurations have zero core energy. Assign unordered sets of \\(m_0\\) labels to centers and choose one deterministic ordering of each set for the root and vertices. Distinct assignments give disjoint integration regions, and relative coordinates have Jacobian one. Thus\n\\[\nZ_{mP,m_0k}^{\\psi_t}(\\beta)\n\\ge\n\\left(\n\\frac{v_0(v_1e^{-3t})^{m_0-1}}{m_0!}\n\\right)^k.\n\\]\nFirst let \\(m\\to\\infty\\), then \\(t\\to\\infty\\). The contribution to \\(q_t\\) has lower limit\n\\[\n\\frac{M_\\ell}{P^3}\\bigl(m_0h-3(m_0-1)\\bigr),\n\\qquad m_0=1,4.\n\\]\nThe empty configuration supplies zero. Let \\(\\ell\\to\\infty\\) and \\(\\eta\\downarrow0\\). This proves pointwise convergence. Proposition \\(1\\) gives local uniform convergence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 9", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The ideal transform and maximizing densities (paper:", "statement": "<p>**The ideal transform and maximizing densities (paper: Lemma 4.2).**</p><p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Continue the varying-\\(t\\) setting of Proposition \\(8\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Put\n  \\[\n  H_t=H_{\\psi_t},\\qquad P_1^t=T_Aq_t,\n  \\qquad A=\\frac3{2p}.\n  \\]</p><p>Statement. Uniformly on compact subsets of \\(\\Omega\\),\n\\[\nP_1^t(\\beta,h)\\longrightarrow\nP_*(\\beta,h)\n:=\n\\max\\left(\n0,\\,\nph+\\frac{\\beta Ap^2}{2},\\,\n4ph-9p+8\\beta Ap^2\n\\right). \\tag{4.4}\n\\]\nMoreover, if \\(t_j\\to\\infty\\), \\((\\beta_j,h_j)\\to(\\beta,h)\\in\\Omega\\), and \\(d_j\\) maximizes the expression defining \\(P_1^{t_j}(\\beta_j,h_j)\\), then \\((d_j)\\) is bounded. Every subsequential limit belongs to \\(\\{0,p,4p\\}\\) and maximizes the corresponding limiting expression.</p>", "proof": "<p>For the pure core, the first inequality in (2.5) gives (2.8) with\n\\[\n\\kappa=t^2b^3/8,\\qquad B_0=t^2/2.\n\\]\nConsequently,\n\\[\nH_t(\\beta,d)\n\\ge\n\\beta t\\left(\\frac{b^3d^2}{8}-\\frac d2\\right)\n+\\frac{d\\log d-d}{t}.\n\\]\nFor large \\(t\\), this quadratic dominates the added term \\(\\beta Ad^2/2\\). On a fixed compact parameter set, the transformed objective is negative for \\(d\\ge D\\), with \\(D\\) independent of all large \\(t\\). Since \\(d=0\\) gives zero, all maximizers lie in \\([0,D]\\).</p><p>The partial conjugate of \\(q_*\\) is\n\\[\nH_*(d)=\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&amp;0\\le d\\le p,\\\\\n3(d-p),&amp;p\\le d\\le4p,\\\\\n+\\infty,&amp;\\text{otherwise}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]</p><p>Pass to a subsequence with \\(d_j\\to d\\). For every \\(z\\in\\mathbb R\\), Fenchel’s inequality gives\n\\[\nP_1^{t_j}(\\beta_j,h_j)\n\\le\nq_{t_j}(\\beta_j,z)+(h_j-z)d_j\n+\\frac{\\beta_jAd_j^2}{2}.\n\\]\nTake the upper limit, then the infimum over \\(z\\). The result is bounded by\n\\[\nhd-H_*(d)+\\frac{\\beta Ad^2}{2}.\n\\]\nIn particular \\(d\\in[0,4p]\\), since the transform is nonnegative. This objective is strictly convex on each of the two linearity intervals of \\(H_*\\); its maximum is therefore \\(P_*(\\beta,h)\\).</p><p>For the matching lower bound, choose a maximizing \\(d_0\\in\\{0,p,4p\\}\\). Its global optimality gives, for every \\(d'\\),\n\\[\nH_*(d')\n\\ge\nH_*(d_0)+(h+\\beta Ad_0)(d'-d_0)\n+\\frac{\\beta A}{2}(d'-d_0)^2. \\tag{4.5}\n\\]\nThus \\(z=h+\\beta Ad_0\\) exposes the unique conjugate density \\(d_0\\) for \\(q_*\\).</p><p>Set \\(z_j=h_j+\\beta_jAd_0\\), and choose\n\\[\n(s_j',d_j')\\in\\partial q_{t_j}(\\beta_j,z_j).\n\\]\nSince the physical pressure is nondecreasing in \\(h\\), \\(d_j'\\ge0\\). By Proposition \\(8\\), Proposition \\(1\\), and (4.5), every cluster is \\((0,d_0)\\). Fenchel equality at \\(d_j'\\) gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_1^{t_j}(\\beta_j,h_j)\n&amp;\\ge\nq_{t_j}(\\beta_j,z_j)\n+(h_j-z_j)d_j'\n+\\frac{\\beta_jA(d_j')^2}{2}\\\\\n&amp;\\longrightarrow P_*(\\beta,h).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe upper and lower limits agree. Equality in the upper argument forces every limiting maximizing density to maximize the limiting objective. Strict convexity on the two intervals confines it to \\(\\{0,p,4p\\}\\). The moving-parameter argument proves all assertions, including local uniform convergence.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 10", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Supports under a quadratic transform (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Supports under a quadratic transform (paper: Lemma 4.4).**</p><p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix \\(t\\) satisfying (2.6), let \\(Q=Q_\\phi\\) for \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\), and put \\(H=H_Q\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(0&lt;a\\le A+1\\), and let \\(F=T_aQ\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose \\(d\\ge0\\) attains the supremum defining \\(F(\\beta,h)\\) at \\((\\beta,h)\\in\\Omega\\). Put \\(z=h+\\beta ad\\).</p><p class=\"li\">For a finite concave function of \\(\\beta\\), write \\(\\partial_\\beta^+\\) for its superdifferential. Thus\n  \\[\n  e\\in-\\partial_\\beta^+H(\\beta_0,d)\n  \\]\n  means\n  \\[\n  H(\\beta,d)\\le H(\\beta_0,d)-e(\\beta-\\beta_0)\n  \\qquad(\\beta&gt;0).\n  \\]</p><p>Statement. For every \\(d'\\ge0\\),\n\\[\nH(\\beta,d')\n\\ge\nH(\\beta,d)+z(d'-d)+\\frac{\\beta a}{2}(d'-d)^2. \\tag{4.8}\n\\]\nThe finite concave function \\(H(\\cdot,d)\\) has a supergradient at \\(\\beta\\). For every \\(e\\in-\\partial_\\beta^+H(\\beta,d)\\),\n\\[\n(e,d)\\in\\partial Q(\\beta,z),\n\\qquad\n\\left(e+\\frac{ad^2}{2},d\\right)\\in\\partial F(\\beta,h). \\tag{4.9}\n\\]\nIf \\(F\\) is differentiable at \\((\\beta,h)\\), the second vector equals \\(\\nabla F(\\beta,h)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Compare the transformed objective at \\(d\\) with its value at \\(d'\\), and complete the square to obtain (4.8). Dropping the nonnegative quadratic shows that \\(z\\) supports \\(H(\\beta,\\cdot)\\) at \\(d\\), so Fenchel equality holds.</p><p>By Proposition \\(3\\), \\(H(\\cdot,d)\\) is finite and concave; it has a supergradient at the interior point \\(\\beta\\). Lemma \\(10.1\\) gives the first inclusion in (4.9).</p><p>For the second inclusion, the fixed-density branch\n\\[\n(\\beta',h')\n\\longmapsto\nh'd-H(\\beta',d)+\\frac{\\beta'ad^2}{2}\n\\]\nlies below \\(F\\) and agrees with it at \\((\\beta,h)\\). The canonical supergradient \\(-e\\) gives this branch supporting vector\n\\[\n\\left(e+\\frac{ad^2}{2},d\\right),\n\\]\nwhich therefore supports \\(F\\). At a differentiability point, the supporting vector is unique.</p>"}, {"label": "Lemma 10.1", "orig": "", "depth": 1, "snippet": "**Joint Fenchel supports (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Joint Fenchel supports (paper: Lemma 4.3).**</p><p>Updated assumptions. Replace the physical-pressure assumption of Proposition \\(10\\) by the following general assumptions:</p><p class=\"li\">\\(Q\\) is finite and convex on \\(\\Omega\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(H(\\beta,d)=\\sup_{h\\in\\mathbb R}\\{hd-Q(\\beta,h)\\}\\);</p><p class=\"li\">\\(d\\ge0\\) is fixed and \\(H(\\cdot,d)\\) is finite and concave;</p><p class=\"li\">\\((\\beta_0,z)\\in\\Omega\\) satisfies\n  \\[\n  H(\\beta_0,d)=zd-Q(\\beta_0,z).\n  \\]</p><span class=\"lbl sub\">Statement</span><p>\\[\n\\{e\\in\\mathbb R:(e,d)\\in\\partial Q(\\beta_0,z)\\}\n=\n-\\partial_\\beta^+H(\\beta_0,d). \\tag{4.7}\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>For \\(e\\) in the right side and every \\((\\beta,h)\\in\\Omega\\), the defining conjugate inequality gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(\\beta,h)\n&amp;\\ge hd-H(\\beta,d)\\\\\n&amp;\\ge Q(\\beta_0,z)+e(\\beta-\\beta_0)+d(h-z).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis is the joint supporting inequality.</p><p>Conversely, a support \\((e,d)\\) gives\n\\[\nhd-Q(\\beta,h)\n\\le\nzd-Q(\\beta_0,z)-e(\\beta-\\beta_0)\n\\]\nfor every \\(h\\). Take the supremum in \\(h\\) to obtain the supergradient inequality for \\(H(\\cdot,d)\\).</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 11", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**The initial supporting triple (paper:", "statement": "<p>**The initial supporting triple (paper: Proposition 4.5).**</p><p>Definitions and geometric choices.</p><p class=\"li\">The three planes defining \\(P_*\\) in Proposition \\(9\\) meet at\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_*=(1,-3/4).\n  \\]\n  Their gradients are\n  \\[\n  v_1=(0,0),\\qquad\n  v_2=(Ap^2/2,p)=(3p/4,p),\\qquad\n  v_3=(8Ap^2,4p)=(12p,4p).\n  \\]\n  They are noncollinear, since \\(\\det(v_2,v_3)=-9p^2\\ne0\\). Put\n  \\[\n  g=\\frac{v_1+v_2+v_3}{3}\n  =\\left(\\frac{17p}{4},\\frac{5p}{3}\\right).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Choose \\(\\tau&gt;0\\) so small that the closed balls\n  \\[\n  U_i=\\overline B(v_i,4\\tau),\\qquad i=1,2,3,\n  \\]\n  have strictly ordered disjoint density projections, with the second projection contained in \\((0,\\infty)\\), and:\n  every triangle with one vertex in each \\(U_i\\) is noncollinear and contains the same ball \\(\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\), for some \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\);\n  and \\(g\\) belongs to the convex hull of no two \\(U_i\\).\n  Such a choice follows from the strict interior position of \\(g\\) in the original triangle and compactness.</p><p class=\"li\">Set\n  \\[\n  r_0=\\frac14,\\qquad W=\\overline B(\\lambda_*,r_0)\\subset\\Omega.\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(P_1^t=T_AQ_{\\psi_t}\\).</p><p>Statement. For all sufficiently large \\(t\\), there are\n\\[\n\\lambda_1\\in B(\\lambda_*,r_0/16)\n\\]\nand nonempty compact convex sets\n\\[\nK_i^1\\subset\\overline B(v_i,\\tau/2),\n\\qquad i=1,2,3,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\ng\\in\\partial P_1^t(\\lambda_1)\n=\n\\operatorname{conv}(K_1^1\\cup K_2^1\\cup K_3^1). \\tag{4.12}\n\\]\nAll their vectors have nonnegative density coordinate. In particular,\n\\[\n\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\subset\\partial P_1^t(\\lambda_1).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>The function \\(P_*(y)-g\\cdot y\\) has its unique minimum at \\(\\lambda_*\\). In fact,\n\\[\nP_*(y)-P_*(\\lambda_*)-g\\cdot(y-\\lambda_*)\n=\n\\max_i(v_i-g)\\cdot(y-\\lambda_*)\n\\ge\\gamma_0|y-\\lambda_*|\n\\]\nfor some \\(\\gamma_0&gt;0\\), because the triangle of the \\(v_i\\) contains a ball about \\(g\\).</p><p>Minimize \\(P_1^t(y)-g\\cdot y\\) over \\(\\overline B(\\lambda_*,r_0/16)\\). Proposition \\(9\\) shows that all minimizers lie in the interior for large \\(t\\) and converge to \\(\\lambda_*\\). Choose one and call it \\(\\lambda_1^t\\). Interior minimality gives\n\\[\ng\\in\\partial P_1^t(\\lambda_1^t).\n\\]</p><p>We claim that any limit of gradients\n\\[\n\\nabla P_1^{t_j}(\\beta_j,h_j),\n\\]\nat differentiability points with \\(t_j\\to\\infty\\) and \\((\\beta_j,h_j)\\to\\lambda_*\\), belongs to \\(\\{v_1,v_2,v_3\\}\\).</p><p>Choose a maximizing density \\(d_j\\). Apply Proposition \\(10\\) with \\(Q=q_{t_j}\\), \\(a=A\\), and choose \\(e_j\\in-\\partial_\\beta^+H_{t_j}(\\beta_j,d_j)\\). With \\(z_j=h_j+\\beta_jAd_j\\),\n\\[\n\\nabla P_1^{t_j}(\\beta_j,h_j)\n=\n\\left(e_j+\\frac{Ad_j^2}{2},d_j\\right), \\tag{4.13}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(e_j,d_j)\\in\\partial q_{t_j}(\\beta_j,z_j). \\tag{4.14}\n\\]\nThe maximizing densities are bounded by Proposition \\(9\\), so \\(z_j\\) stays in a compact interval. The locally uniform limit \\(q_*\\) is independent of \\(\\beta\\); hence every cluster of the subgradients in (4.14) has first coordinate zero. Thus \\(e_j\\to0\\). Proposition \\(9\\) puts every density cluster in \\(\\{0,p,4p\\}\\), and (4.13) proves the claim.</p><p>Subgradients of \\(P_1^t\\) near \\(\\lambda_*\\) are uniformly bounded for large \\(t\\), by Proposition \\(9\\). If\n\\[\nG_{P_1^t}(\\lambda_1^t)\n\\not\\subset\n\\bigcup_i\\overline B(v_i,\\tau/2)\n\\]\nfor arbitrarily large \\(t\\), choose such reachable gradients. Approximate each by a gradient at a differentiability point within \\(1/t\\) of \\(\\lambda_1^t\\), with gradient error tending to zero. A bounded subsequence contradicts the preceding claim.</p><p>Thus, for large \\(t\\), the three intersections\n\\[\nG_{P_1^t}(\\lambda_1^t)\\cap\\overline B(v_i,\\tau/2)\n\\]\ncover the reachable-gradient set. Let \\(K_i^1\\) be their convex hulls. They are compact and stay in the respective balls. Proposition \\(1\\) gives (4.12).</p><p>None is empty: otherwise \\(g\\) would belong to the convex hull of two larger balls \\(U_i\\). Their density coordinates are nonnegative because \\(P_1^t\\) is nondecreasing in \\(h\\).</p><p>Choose one point in each \\(K_i^1\\). Their triangle contains \\(\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\) by the fixed geometry and lies in \\(\\partial P_1^t(\\lambda_1^t)\\). This proves the last assertion.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 12", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**Persistence of three gradient groups (paper:", "statement": "<p>**Persistence of three gradient groups (paper: Lemma 5.1).**</p><p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix one \\(t\\) satisfying (2.6) and Proposition \\(11\\). A physical pressure means \\(Q=Q_\\phi\\) with \\(\\phi\\in\\mathcal C_t\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(W\\subset\\Omega\\) be compact, let \\(P\\) be finite and convex on \\(\\Omega\\), and let \\(\\lambda_0=(\\beta_0,h_0)\\in\\operatorname{int}W\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Suppose\n  \\[\n  \\partial P(\\lambda_0)\n  =K=\\operatorname{conv}(K_1\\cup K_2\\cup K_3),\n  \\qquad \\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\subset K, \\tag{5.2}\n  \\]\n  where \\(\\gamma&gt;0\\) and the \\(K_i\\) are nonempty compact convex sets with nonnegative, strictly ordered density projections.</p><p class=\"li\">Let \\(\\zeta&gt;0\\) be such that \\(K_i+\\overline B(0,\\zeta)\\) still have disjoint density projections, and \\(g\\) lies outside the convex hull of any two of those enlarged sets.</p><p class=\"li\">For \\(\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^2\\), let\n  \\[\n  F_\\xi=\\{v\\in K:\\xi\\cdot v=h_K(\\xi)\\}.\n  \\]\n  In particular \\(F_0=K\\), and \\(\\partial h_K(\\xi)=F_\\xi\\).</p><p>Statement. There are \\(S&lt;\\infty\\) and \\(a_*&gt;0\\) such that, for every \\(0&lt;a&lt;a_*\\) and every physical pressure \\(Q\\) satisfying\n\\[\n\\|Q-P\\|_W\\le a^2, \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\nthere is \\(\\lambda'\\in\\operatorname{int}W\\), with\n\\[\n|\\lambda'-\\lambda_0|\\le Sa,\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\ng\\in\\partial(T_aQ)(\\lambda')\n=\n\\operatorname{conv}(J_1\\cup J_2\\cup J_3). \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\nEach \\(J_i\\) is nonempty compact convex, has nonnegative density projection, and satisfies\n\\[\nJ_i\\subset K_i+\\overline B(0,\\zeta).\n\\]\nThe constants may depend on the displayed data and the fixed physical class, but not on \\(Q\\). One may require \\(a_*\\le\\min(1,A+1)\\).</p>", "proof": "<p>Prove the conclusion along an arbitrary sequence \\(a_m\\downarrow0\\) and physical pressures \\(Q_m\\) with\n\\[\n\\|Q_m-P\\|_W\\le a_m^2.\n\\]\nThe radius \\(S\\) will depend only on the fixed limiting data. A contradiction argument will then give one threshold \\(a_*\\).</p><p>Write \\(\\xi=(x,y)\\), let \\(\\pi_2(s,d)=d\\), and define\n\\[\nr(x,y)\n=\n\\max_{(s,d)\\in K}\n\\left(xs+yd+\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}d^2\\right). \\tag{5.5}\n\\]</p><p>Every maximizer of this expression over \\(K\\) belongs to some \\(K_i\\). Indeed, write a maximizer as a finite convex combination\n\\[\nv=\\sum_j\\theta_jv_j,\\qquad\nv_j=(s_j,d_j)\\in\\bigcup_iK_i,\n\\]\nwith positive weights. If two densities differ, strict convexity gives\n\\[\n\\xi\\cdot v+\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}(\\pi_2v)^2\n&lt;\n\\sum_j\\theta_j\n\\left(\\xi\\cdot v_j+\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}d_j^2\\right)\n\\le r(\\xi),\n\\]\na contradiction. All densities therefore coincide. Disjoint projections put all \\(v_j\\) in one \\(K_i\\), and convexity gives \\(v\\in K_i\\).</p><p>At scale \\(a_m\\), on\n\\[\n\\Omega_m=\\{\\xi:\\lambda_0+a_m\\xi\\in\\Omega\\},\n\\]\nput\n\\[\nq_m(\\xi)\n=\n\\frac{Q_m(\\lambda_0+a_m\\xi)-P(\\lambda_0)}{a_m}. \\tag{5.6}\n\\]\nEvery fixed compact set of scaled coordinates maps into \\(\\operatorname{int}W\\) for large \\(m\\). There, \\(q_m\\) differs by at most \\(a_m\\) from the corresponding rescaling of \\(P\\). Proposition \\(1\\) identifies the latter limit as the directional derivative \\(h_K\\), uniformly on compact sets. Hence\n\\[\nq_m\\longrightarrow h_K\n\\qquad\\text{locally uniformly on }\\mathbb R^2. \\tag{5.7}\n\\]\nScaling preserves subgradients:\n\\[\n\\partial q_m(\\xi)=\\partial Q_m(\\lambda_0+a_m\\xi).\n\\]\nThus, if \\(\\xi_m\\to\\xi\\) and\n\\[\nv_m\\in\\partial Q_m(\\lambda_0+a_m\\xi_m),\n\\]\nthen \\((v_m)\\) is bounded and every limit belongs to \\(F_\\xi\\).</p><p>Now put\n\\[\nB_m(\\xi)\n=\n\\frac{T_{a_m}Q_m(\\lambda_0+a_m\\xi)-P(\\lambda_0)}{a_m}.\n\\]\nWe prove\n\\[\nB_m\\longrightarrow r\n\\qquad\\text{locally uniformly on }\\mathbb R^2. \\tag{5.8}\n\\]</p><p>For fixed \\(x\\), define\n\\[\nk_x(d)=\\max\\{xs:(s,d)\\in K\\},\n\\]\nwith value \\(-\\infty\\) outside the density projection. This function is upper semicontinuous and concave on its compact interval of finite values. Proposition \\(1\\) gives\n\\[\nk_x(d)=\\inf_{z\\in\\mathbb R}\\{h_K(x,z)-zd\\}. \\tag{5.9}\n\\]</p><p>We retain optimizer information. If \\(\\xi_m=(x_m,y_m)\\to(x,y)\\) and \\(d_m\\) maximizes the transform at \\(\\lambda_0+a_m\\xi_m\\), then every density limit \\(d\\) will satisfy\n\\[\nd\\in\\pi_2K,\\qquad\nr(x,y)=k_x(d)+yd+\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}d^2. \\tag{5.10}\n\\]\nWrite \\(\\beta_m=\\beta_0+a_mx_m\\) and \\(h_m=h_0+a_my_m\\). Proposition \\(4\\) bounds \\(d_m\\). For every fixed \\(z\\), Fenchel’s inequality at \\((\\beta_m,h_0+a_mz)\\) gives\n\\[\nB_m(\\xi_m)\n\\le\nq_m(x_m,z)+(y_m-z)d_m+\\frac{\\beta_m}{2}d_m^2. \\tag{5.11}\n\\]</p><p>For the lower bound, choose \\(v_*=(s_*,d_*)\\in K\\) attaining \\(r(x,y)\\), and put \\(z=y+\\beta_0d_*\\). Comparing its value with any \\((s',d')\\in K\\) and completing the square gives\n\\[\nx(s_*-s')+z(d_*-d')\n\\ge\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}(d'-d_*)^2. \\tag{5.12}\n\\]\nThus \\(v_*\\in F_{(x,z)}\\), and every member of this face has density \\(d_*\\).</p><p>Choose a full subgradient of \\(Q_m\\) at \\((\\beta_m,h_0+a_mz)\\), with density coordinate \\(d_m'\\). The scaled subgradient conclusion gives \\(d_m'\\to d_*\\). Monotonicity in \\(h\\) gives \\(d_m'\\ge0\\). Fenchel equality permits evaluation of the transformed objective:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_m(\\xi_m)\n&amp;\\ge\nq_m(x_m,z)+(y_m-z)d_m'\n+\\frac{\\beta_m}{2}(d_m')^2\\\\\n&amp;\\longrightarrow\nxs_*+yd_*+\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}d_*^2\n=r(x,y). \\qquad(5.13)\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]</p><p>Pass to a subsequence in (5.11) with \\(d_m\\to d\\), take limits, and then infimize over \\(z\\). By (5.9),\n\\[\n\\limsup_m B_m(\\xi_m)\n\\le\nk_x(d)+yd+\\frac{\\beta_0}{2}d^2\n\\le r(x,y).\n\\]\nThe finite lower bound excludes \\(d\\notin\\pi_2K\\). Equality proves (5.10). The argument works for every convergent parameter sequence in a fixed compact set, proving (5.8).</p><p>Next locate a nearby point supporting \\(g\\). Since \\(\\beta_0&gt;0\\),\n\\[\nr(\\xi)-g\\cdot\\xi\n\\ge h_K(\\xi)-g\\cdot\\xi\n\\ge\\gamma|\\xi|. \\tag{5.14}\n\\]\nChoose \\(S&gt;0\\) with \\(\\gamma S&gt;r(0)+1\\). Let \\(\\xi_m\\) minimize\n\\[\nB_m(\\xi)-g\\cdot\\xi\n\\]\non \\(\\overline B(0,S)\\). Uniform convergence and (5.14) imply that all these minimizers are interior for large \\(m\\); every limit is also interior. Hence, at\n\\[\n\\lambda_m'=\\lambda_0+a_m\\xi_m,\n\\]\nconvex minimality gives\n\\[\ng\\in\\partial(T_{a_m}Q_m)(\\lambda_m'),\n\\qquad\n|\\lambda_m'-\\lambda_0|\\le Sa_m. \\tag{5.15}\n\\]\nFor large \\(m\\), these points lie in \\(\\operatorname{int}W\\).</p><p>Put\n\\[\nG_m=G_{T_{a_m}Q_m}(\\lambda_m').\n\\]\nWe claim that eventually\n\\[\nG_m\\subset\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^3\n\\bigl(K_i+\\overline B(0,\\zeta/2)\\bigr). \\tag{5.16}\n\\]\nOtherwise choose \\(u_m\\in G_m\\) outside this union along a subsequence. There is a differentiability point \\(\\widehat\\lambda_m\\) of \\(T_{a_m}Q_m\\) such that\n\\[\n|\\widehat\\lambda_m-\\lambda_m'|&lt;a_m^2,\n\\qquad\n|\\nabla(T_{a_m}Q_m)(\\widehat\\lambda_m)-u_m|&lt;1/m.\n\\]\nWrite\n\\[\n\\widehat\\lambda_m=\\lambda_0+a_m\\widehat\\xi_m.\n\\]\nThe scaled points stay in a fixed compact set, and\n\\[\n\\nabla B_m(\\widehat\\xi_m)\n=\n\\nabla(T_{a_m}Q_m)(\\widehat\\lambda_m).\n\\]\nTheir boundedness follows from the local uniform convex convergence (5.8). Pass to a subsequence with\n\\[\n\\xi_m\\to\\xi=(x,y),\\qquad\n\\widehat\\xi_m\\to\\xi,\\qquad\nu_m\\to u=(s,d).\n\\]</p><p>Let \\(d_m\\) be any maximizing density at\n\\[\n\\widehat\\lambda_m=(\\widehat\\beta_m,\\widehat h_m).\n\\]\nProposition \\(10\\), with \\(a=a_m\\), permits a choice\n\\[\ne_m\\in-\\partial_\\beta^+H_{Q_m}(\\widehat\\beta_m,d_m)\n\\]\nsuch that\n\\[\n\\nabla(T_{a_m}Q_m)(\\widehat\\lambda_m)\n=\n\\left(e_m+\\frac{a_m}{2}d_m^2,d_m\\right), \\tag{5.17}\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n(e_m,d_m)\\in\n\\partial Q_m\n(\\widehat\\beta_m,\\widehat h_m+\\widehat\\beta_ma_md_m). \\tag{5.18}\n\\]\nThus \\(d_m\\to d\\), and the first-coordinate increment tends to zero. The shifted argument has scaled coordinates converging to\n\\[\n\\eta=(x,y+\\beta_0d).\n\\]\nThe scaled subgradient conclusion shows \\(u\\in F_\\eta\\).</p><p>By (5.10), there is \\(v_*=(s_*,d)\\in K\\) maximizing the expression defining \\(r(x,y)\\). Equation (5.12) gives \\(v_*\\in F_\\eta\\). The vectors \\(u,v_*\\) have the same density and the same value against \\(\\eta\\), so \\(xs=xs_*\\). Therefore \\(u\\) itself maximizes that expression, also when \\(x=0\\). The initial geometric observation puts \\(u\\) in one \\(K_i\\), contradicting the choice of \\(u_m\\). This proves (5.16).</p><p>Define\n\\[\nJ_{i,m}\n=\n\\operatorname{conv}\n\\left(\nG_m\\cap\n(K_i+\\overline B(0,\\zeta/2))\n\\right).\n\\]\nReachable-gradient sets are compact. Hence every nonempty \\(J_{i,m}\\) is compact convex and lies in the indicated neighborhood. Proposition \\(1\\) and (5.16) give\n\\[\n\\partial(T_{a_m}Q_m)(\\lambda_m')\n=\n\\operatorname{conv}(J_{1,m}\\cup J_{2,m}\\cup J_{3,m}).\n\\]\nNone is empty: otherwise (5.15) puts \\(g\\) in the convex hull of two allowed enlarged groups. Every density coordinate is nonnegative because \\(T_{a_m}Q_m\\) is nondecreasing in \\(h\\).</p><p>The conclusion holds for all sufficiently large indices along every sequence \\(a_m\\downarrow0\\), with the same radius \\(S\\). If no uniform threshold existed, a sequence of counterexamples with \\(a_m\\downarrow0\\) would contradict this. Decrease \\(a_*\\) so that\n\\[\na_*\\le\\min(1,A+1),\n\\qquad\n\\lambda_0+\\overline B(0,Sa_*)\\subset\\operatorname{int}W.\n\\]\nThis completes the proof.</p>"}, {"label": "Proposition 13", "orig": "", "depth": 0, "snippet": "**One radial potential carrying a common ball of pressure slopes (paper:", "statement": "<p>**One radial potential carrying a common ball of pressure slopes (paper: Sections 6.1–6.3).**</p><p>Assumptions and definitions.</p><p class=\"li\">Fix one \\(t\\) satisfying (2.6) and Proposition \\(11\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Retain \\(A,p,\\psi_t,w\\), the slope vectors \\(v_i\\), the sets \\(U_i\\), the center \\(g\\), the radius \\(\\gamma\\), and\n  \\[\n  \\lambda_*=(1,-3/4),\\qquad\n  r_0=1/4,\\qquad\n  W=\\overline B(\\lambda_*,r_0).\n  \\]</p><p class=\"li\">Let\n  \\[\n  q_0=Q_{\\psi_t},\\qquad \\sigma_1=A,\\qquad P_1=T_Aq_0,\n  \\]\n  with initial \\(\\lambda_1,K_i^1\\) from Proposition \\(11\\).</p><p class=\"li\">Choose positive sequences \\((\\zeta_n)\\), \\((\\ell_n)\\) satisfying\n  \\[\n  \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\zeta_n&lt;\\tau,\\qquad\n  \\sum_{n\\ge1}\\ell_n&lt;r_0/16. \\tag{6.1}\n  \\]</p><p>Statement. There exist positive masses \\(\\sigma_n\\), ranges \\(R_n&gt;4\\) with \\(R_n&gt;R_{n-1}+1\\) for \\(n&gt;1\\), physical pressures\n\\[\n\\phi_n=\\psi_t-t\\sum_{k\\le n}\\sigma_kw_{R_k},\n\\qquad q_n=Q_{\\phi_n},\n\\]\nand ideal pressures\n\\[\nP_n=T_{\\sigma_n}q_{n-1},\n\\]\nwith points \\(\\lambda_n\\) and nonempty compact convex groups \\(K_i^n\\), such that\n\\[\n\\sum_{n\\ge2}\\sigma_n&lt;1,\\qquad\n|\\lambda_{n+1}-\\lambda_n|\\le\\ell_n,\n\\]\n\\[\ng\\in\\partial P_n(\\lambda_n)\n=\n\\operatorname{conv}\\left(\\bigcup_{i=1}^3K_i^n\\right),\n\\qquad\nK_i^n\\subset\n\\overline B\\left(v_i,\\frac\\tau2+\\sum_{k&lt;n}\\zeta_k\\right),\n\\]\nall density coordinates in \\(K_i^n\\) are nonnegative, and\n\\[\n\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\subset\\partial P_n(\\lambda_n).\n\\]\nMoreover,\n\\[\n\\|q_n-P_n\\|_W\n\\le\\min\\{\\sigma_{n+1}^2,2^{-n}\\}. \\tag{6.5}\n\\]</p><p>The single potential\n\\[\n\\Phi=\\psi_t-t\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\sigma_jw_{R_j} \\tag{6.6}\n\\]\nis admissible and satisfies \\(\\varphi(r)=o(r^{-3})\\). Its physical pressure \\(Q_\\infty=Q_\\Phi\\) satisfies\n\\[\n\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\n\\subset\\partial Q_\\infty(\\lambda_c), \\tag{6.8}\n\\]\nwhere\n\\[\n\\lambda_n\\to\\lambda_c=(\\beta_c,h_c)\n\\in B(\\lambda_*,r_0/8).\n\\]</p>", "proof": "<p>Suppose\n\\[\n\\phi_{n-1}=\\psi_t-t\\sum_{k&lt;n}\\sigma_kw_{R_k},\n\\qquad\nq_{n-1}=Q_{\\phi_{n-1}},\n\\qquad\nP_n=T_{\\sigma_n}q_{n-1}\n\\]\nhave been defined, with\n\\[\ng\\in\\partial P_n(\\lambda_n)\n=\n\\operatorname{conv}\\left(\\bigcup_iK_i^n\\right),\n\\]\nand\n\\[\nK_i^n\n\\subset\n\\overline B\\left(v_i,\\frac\\tau2+\\sum_{k&lt;n}\\zeta_k\\right).\n\\]\nThe fixed geometry gives\n\\[\n\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\subset\\partial P_n(\\lambda_n).\n\\]\nThe enlarged groups \\(K_i^n+\\overline B(0,\\zeta_n)\\) lie in \\(U_i\\); their density projections are disjoint, and the convex hull of any two does not contain \\(g\\).</p><p>Apply Proposition \\(12\\) to \\(P_n,\\lambda_n,\\zeta_n\\), and denote its constants by \\(S_n,a_n^*\\). First choose\n\\[\n0&lt;\\sigma_{n+1}\n&lt;\n\\min\\left\\{\na_n^*,\\,\n\\frac{\\ell_n}{\\max\\{1,S_n\\}},\\,\n2^{-n-1}\n\\right\\}. \\tag{6.4}\n\\]\nOnly afterward choose \\(R_n&gt;4\\), and \\(R_n&gt;R_{n-1}+1\\) when \\(n&gt;1\\), large enough that Proposition \\(7\\), applied to the fixed finite-range reference \\(\\phi_{n-1}\\), gives\n\\[\n\\phi_n=\\phi_{n-1}-t\\sigma_nw_{R_n},\n\\qquad\nq_n=Q_{\\phi_n},\n\\]\nand\n\\[\n\\|q_n-P_n\\|_W\n\\le\\min\\{\\sigma_{n+1}^2,2^{-n}\\}.\n\\]\nThe physical class is respected because \\(\\sum_{k\\ge2}\\sigma_k&lt;1\\).</p><p>Proposition \\(12\\), now applied to \\(q_n\\) with \\(a=\\sigma_{n+1}\\), gives\n\\[\nP_{n+1}=T_{\\sigma_{n+1}}q_n,\n\\]\na point \\(\\lambda_{n+1}\\), and groups \\(K_i^{n+1}\\) with\n\\[\n|\\lambda_{n+1}-\\lambda_n|\\le\\ell_n,\\qquad\nK_i^{n+1}\\subset K_i^n+\\overline B(0,\\zeta_n).\n\\]\nThis preserves the induction hypotheses. The initial location and (6.1) keep every \\(\\lambda_n\\) in \\(B(\\lambda_*,r_0/8)\\).</p><p>The order of the choices is essential: the next mass fixes the required precision of the current physical approximation. Each reference is fixed before the corresponding range is chosen; no uniform Kac convergence rate over changing references is needed.</p><p>Define \\(\\Phi\\) by (6.6). Since \\(R_j\\ge1\\) and \\(\\sum_j\\sigma_j&lt;\\infty\\), the attractive series converges uniformly and in \\(L^1(\\mathbb R^3)\\). It is radial and nonnegative before subtraction. Thus \\(\\Phi\\) is Borel measurable, finite and locally bounded away from the origin, and has the same divergence there as \\(\\psi_t\\).</p><p>The core vanishes for \\(|x|\\ge1\\), so\n\\[\n4\\pi\\int_1^\\infty r^2|\\varphi(r)|\\,dr\n=\n\\int_{|x|\\ge1}|\\Phi(x)|\\,dx&lt;\\infty.\n\\]\nCompact support of \\(w\\) also gives, for \\(r\\ge1\\),\n\\[\nr^3|\\varphi(r)|\n\\le\nt\\|w\\|_\\infty\\sum_{R_j\\ge r}\\sigma_j\n\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\nThis proves \\(o(r^{-3})\\), without imposing a power-law relation between \\(\\sigma_j\\) and \\(R_j\\).</p><p>Because \\(R_1&gt;4\\), \\(w\\) has a positive minimum on \\(|y|\\le2/R_1\\). Since \\(\\sigma_1=A&gt;0\\),\n\\[\n\\Phi(x)\n\\le\n-tAR_1^{-3}\n\\min_{|y|\\le2/R_1}w(y)\n&lt;0\n\\qquad(1\\le|x|\\le2).\n\\]\nProposition \\(2\\) applies to the infinite sum and gives\n\\[\nU_N^\\Phi\\ge-B_0N.\n\\]\nHence \\(\\Phi\\) is admissible.</p><p>Let \\(Q_\\infty=Q_\\Phi\\) and \\(H_\\infty=H_\\Phi\\), whose existence, finiteness, and duality follow from Proposition \\(3\\). Proposition \\(6\\) and (6.5) imply\n\\[\n\\|Q_\\infty-P_n\\|_W\n\\le\nC_W\\sum_{k&gt;n}\\sigma_k\n+\\min\\{\\sigma_{n+1}^2,2^{-n}\\}\n\\longrightarrow0. \\tag{6.7}\n\\]\nThus the limiting pressure is the pressure of the single potential \\(\\Phi\\).</p><p>The summable displacement bounds give\n\\[\n\\lambda_n\\to\\lambda_c=(\\beta_c,h_c)\n\\in B(\\lambda_*,r_0/8).\n\\]\nFix \\(v\\in\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\). For every \\(\\lambda\\in\\operatorname{int}W\\),\n\\[\nP_n(\\lambda)\n\\ge\nP_n(\\lambda_n)+v\\cdot(\\lambda-\\lambda_n).\n\\]\nLet \\(n\\to\\infty\\), using (6.7). This gives a local supporting inequality for \\(Q_\\infty\\) at \\(\\lambda_c\\).</p><p>The local support is global on the convex domain \\(\\Omega\\): for any \\(\\lambda\\in\\Omega\\), a short initial portion of the segment from \\(\\lambda_c\\) to \\(\\lambda\\) lies in \\(\\operatorname{int}W\\); combine its supporting inequality with convexity of \\(Q_\\infty\\). Therefore\n\\[\n\\overline B(g,\\gamma)\\subset\\partial Q_\\infty(\\lambda_c).\n\\]</p>"}], "edges": [[2, 1, "part"], [3, 1, "part"], [4, 1, "part"], [6, 5, "part"], [15, 14, "part"]]});
